# install one more tool named kubectl
kubectl is the command-line tool for controlling a Kubernetes cluster. It talks to the cluster’s API server, so you can deploy apps, inspect resources, view logs, and manage the cluster without a GUI.

| curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl" | Installs kubectl | command from k8s official website |

# give executable permissions to this file.
| chmod +x kubectl | add x permission |

| mv kubectl /usr/local/bin/ | moves kubectl to the default path | this is the default path on linux machines |

# We can create any objects in k8s using imperative and declarative methods.
# Imperative (command line)
# Declarative (Manifest files)

# Let's create a pod using imperative method

| kubectl run mypod --image nginx | creates a pod with the name mypod with a container based on nginx image | We should only give an image from dockerhub |

| kubectl get pods | list of pods | can also use kubectl get po |

# Let's create a pod using declarative method

| vim pod.yml | creates and opens yml format file | this can be the manifest file for creating the pods |

 #every object in k8s has an api version

| kubectl api-resources | Gives complete info |
|| kubectl api-resources | grep -i pod || using grep ||

---
apiVersion : v1 
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: pod-2
spec:
  containers:
    - name: cont1
      image: shaikmustafa/dm
      ports:
        - containerPort: 80

| kubectl create -f pod.yml | create a pod named pod-2 | similar command is used to create any object using a yml file | -f represents the file name |

| kubectl delete pod pod-2 | deletes pod named pod-2 |

| kubectl delete pod mypod pod-1 pod-2 | deletes pods named mypod, pod-1 and pod-2 |

| kubectl delete pod --all | deletes all pods |






